※ pl4g-test: compile=ok run=native,qemu exit=0 diag=none

※ A variable at the top level may hold a result.  What one looks like in
※ memory is the answer where an answer goes and one byte beside it saying
※ whether there is one, so reading such a variable is two reads of one place
※ and writing it is two writes.
※
※ The only constant of a result type a program can write is the successful
※ one: a value of the answer type written where a result is wanted is that
※ result, and there is no other way to write one.

let hundred: mut u8 = 100u8
let none: mut u8 = 0u8
let seven: mut u8 = 7u8

※ the successful result holding one, which is how a result is initialized
@[visible]
let kept: mut u8? = 1u8

※ one that nothing assigns to, which goes in the read-only section
@[visible]
let fixed: u8? = 9u8

@[startup, impure]
fn main() → u6:
    ※ a division with no answer, kept and then read back
    kept ← hundred ÷ none
    let after: u8 = kept ?? 50u8
    ※ and one with an answer
    kept ← hundred ÷ seven
    ※ what this used to say through the exit status, which is six bits now
    if after + (kept ?? 0u8) + (fixed ?? 0u8) - 9u8 ≠ 64u8:
        1u6
    else:
        0u6
